---
import ComparisonTable from "@components/article/ComparisonTable.astro";
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import { getArticleBySlug } from "../../data/articles";

// Get article metadata from central data file
const article = getArticleBySlug("crypto-friendly-banks", "blog")!;
const { title, description, readingTime, image, imageAlt } = article;
const pubDate = new Date(article.pubDate);

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  { text: "The Problem", slug: "introduction", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Quick Comparison", slug: "quick-comparison", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Why Banks Hate Crypto", slug: "why-banks-block", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Good Options", slug: "best-options", depth: 2 },
  { text: "How to Not Get Banned", slug: "best-practices", depth: 2 },
  { text: "FAQ", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Bottom Line", slug: "conclusion", depth: 2 },
];

// Comparison table
const comparisonColumns = [
  { key: "bank", label: "Bank", width: "110px" },
  { key: "type", label: "Type" },
  { key: "crypto", label: "Crypto Friendly" },
  { key: "transfers", label: "Exchange Transfers" },
  { key: "notes", label: "Notes" },
];

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    bank: "<strong>Chase</strong>",
    type: "Traditional",
    crypto: "Coinbase partner",
    transfers: "Yes",
    notes: "Card funding + points to USDC",
  },
  {
    bank: "<strong>Ally Bank</strong>",
    type: "Online",
    crypto: "Tolerant",
    transfers: "Yes (ACH)",
    notes: "No crypto held at Ally",
  },
  {
    bank: "<strong>Fidelity</strong>",
    type: "Brokerage",
    crypto: "Supports",
    transfers: "Yes",
    notes: "Bitcoin in retirement accounts",
  },
  {
    bank: "<strong>Mercury</strong>",
    type: "Business (fintech)",
    crypto: "Business OK",
    transfers: "Yes",
    notes: "Crypto companies welcome",
  },
  {
    bank: "<strong>Revolut</strong>",
    type: "Digital",
    crypto: "Built-in (UK/EU only)",
    transfers: "Yes",
    notes: "No crypto for US customers",
  },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Can they close my account for buying crypto?",
    answer:
      "<p>It's less likely than it was. Regulators pulled back their anti-crypto guidance in 2025. But banks still run fraud checks, so big or sudden transfers can still get flagged. Keep it boring.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I tell my bank?",
    answer: "<p>No need to volunteer. If they ask, be honest. Crypto investing is legal.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Best way to cash out?",
    answer:
      "<p>Sell on Coinbase/Kraken/Gemini, withdraw to bank. Smaller regular withdrawals beat one huge transfer. Or use a crypto debit card.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Are crypto banks safe?",
    answer:
      "<p>Be careful with newer ones. Juno's cash accounts got stuck in the Synapse mess in 2024 and the company shut down in 2025. Check who actually holds your money and whether it's FDIC insured. Only keep what you're using.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I pay taxes on crypto?",
    answer:
      "<p>Yes. Treated as property in the US. Capital gains when you sell. Keep records. Use Koinly or CoinTracker.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I use Revolut for crypto in the US?",
    answer:
      "<p>Not right now. Revolut stopped crypto for US customers in October 2023. It's still available in the UK and much of Europe.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best Banking for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Multi-currency accounts and low-fee banking for international living.",
    href: "/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1563986768494-4dee2763ff3f-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Mobile banking app",
  },
  {
    title: "Tax Tips for Remote Workers",
    excerpt: "Navigate taxes including crypto reporting as a digital nomad.",
    href: "/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1554224155-6726b3ff858f-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Tax documents",
  },
  {
    title: "Best VPN for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Secure your crypto transactions while traveling.",
    href: "/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1526374965328-7f61d4dc18c5-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "VPN security",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="blog"
    subcategorySlug="finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">The Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Got locked out of my Chase account in Lisbon. On a Sunday. With rent due Monday.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason? Three transfers to Coinbase the week before. "Suspicious activity." No warning, no appeal. Account frozen.
    Spent 45 minutes on hold from a Portuguese phone number before they'd even talk to me.
  </p>
  <p>
    Crypto is legal. Banks act like it isn't. Transfer money to an exchange? Flagged. Cash out? Frozen. Do it too often?
    Account closed without explanation.
  </p>
  <p>That was the $2,400 lesson. Rent I had to scramble to cover with a backup card. Not all banks are like this. Some tolerate crypto. Some embrace it. A few even have it built in. Here's which ones won't leave you stranded.</p>

  <p>Funny thing. In July 2025 Chase, the same bank, announced a partnership with Coinbase. Chase cards can fund Coinbase accounts now, and Chase points convert to USDC starting in 2026. So yeah. Things change fast.</p>

  <!-- Quick Comparison -->
  <h2 id="quick-comparison">Quick Comparison</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={comparisonColumns} rows={comparisonRows}/>

  <p><strong>My picks:</strong> US: Ally for plain banking that won't hassle your exchange transfers. Chase if you're already there, since it works with Coinbase directly now. Mercury if you're running a crypto business. Outside the US: Revolut.</p>

  <!-- Why Banks Block Crypto -->
  <h2 id="why-banks-block">Why Banks Hate Crypto</h2>
  <p>
    Compliance teams are terrified. Money laundering regulations are strict. Crypto is pseudonymous. Easier to just ban
    everything than risk a regulatory nightmare.
  </p>
  <p>
    They also see fraud reports constantly. Doesn't matter if the exchange wasn't at fault. They get spooked.
  </p>
  <p>
    And sudden large transfers look suspicious to automated systems. $50K appearing overnight? Fraud alert.
  </p>

  <h3>What Changed in 2025</h3>

  <p>The mood flipped. In March 2025 the OCC, which regulates national banks, confirmed that crypto custody and some stablecoin activity are fine for banks, and pulled out of the old warning statements. In August an executive order went after "politicized or unlawful debanking."</p>

  <p>Does that mean no bank will ever flag you again? No. Fraud and anti-money-laundering systems didn't go anywhere. A random $50K showing up overnight still looks weird to a computer. But "we don't do crypto, goodbye" is a lot rarer than it was when I got locked out.</p>

  <p>Bank of America, Wells Fargo and Citi all had reputations for declining or restricting crypto transfers. I'd still keep crypto activity away from your main account there. Policies change. Your mileage may vary.</p>

  <!-- Best Options -->
  <h2 id="best-options">The Good Options</h2>

  <h3>Banks With Crypto Built In</h3>

  <h4 id="revolut">Revolut (UK and Europe, not the US)</h4>
  <p>
    Big caveat first. Revolut stopped offering crypto to US customers in October 2023. They're working on a US bank,
    but nothing's back yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Outside the US it's still the easiest built-in option. Buy and sell in the app, and Revolut X if you want a proper
    exchange with limit orders.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Everything in one place. No suspicious transfers because it's all internal.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> Useless for crypto if you're in the States. And external wallet withdrawals only
    work for some coins and plans.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/revolut" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-teal-ink underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" target="_blank">
      Open Revolut Account (UK/EU) &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h4 id="juno">What happened to Juno</h4>
  <p>
    It shut down in September 2025. Its cash accounts ran through Synapse, the middleware company whose collapse froze
    a lot of fintech accounts in 2024. Which is basically the whole argument for keeping your main money at an actual
    bank.
  </p>

  <h3>Traditional Banks That Don't Care</h3>

  <h4 id="ally">Ally Bank</h4>
  <p>Online bank. You can't buy crypto at Ally and you can't send crypto to it. Dollars only. But linking it to Coinbase or Kraken by ACH generally just works, which is all most people need.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Traditional bank that just... lets you do crypto stuff.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> US only.
  </p>

  <h4 id="fidelity">Fidelity</h4>
  <p>
    Big brokerage embracing crypto. Bitcoin in 401(k)s. Generally friendly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Crypto in <a href="/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers/">retirement accounts</a>. Legitimacy.
  </p>

  <h3>Business Banking</h3>

  <h4 id="mercury">Mercury</h4>
  <p>
    Modern <a href="/guides/freelancer-tax-deductions-guide/">business banking</a> that actually accepts crypto
    companies. Web3 startups, exchanges. They'll work with you.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What I like:</strong> One of the few options if your business involves crypto. Mercury has a whole page for web3 companies. Technically it's a fintech, not a bank. The accounts sit at partner banks with FDIC coverage.</p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/mercury" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-teal-ink underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" target="_blank">
      Open Mercury Account &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3>Crypto-Native Options</h3>
  <p>
    Not banks, but banking-like:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Coinbase Card:</strong> Spend crypto anywhere Visa works</li>
    <li><strong>Crypto.com:</strong> Metal cards, rewards</li>
    <li><strong>Nexo:</strong> Earn interest, borrow against holdings. Back in the US since April 2025, after leaving in 2022.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Warning:</strong> Not FDIC insured. Only keep what you're actively using.
  </p>

  <!-- Best Practices -->
  <h2 id="best-practices">How to Not Get Banned</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Use legit exchanges.</strong> Banks know Coinbase. They don't know random DEX #47.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Avoid huge sudden transfers.</strong> $50K appearing overnight looks sketchy. Spread it out.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong><a href="/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers/">Keep records</a>.</strong> If they ask, you want to show legitimate
    investing, not money laundering.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Use separate accounts.</strong> Keep crypto activity in one bank. If it gets restricted, your main banking
    survives.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Don't lie.</strong> If they ask about transactions, be honest. Lying creates way bigger problems.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Have backups.</strong> Always <a href="/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads/">multiple banks</a>. Never be
    stranded.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/revolut" rel="nofollow sponsored" target="_blank">Revolut</a> if you want crypto built into your banking
    and you're outside the US. Ally if you want traditional banking that tolerates crypto. Chase if you already bank
    there, now that it works with Coinbase.
    <a href="/go/mercury" rel="nofollow sponsored" target="_blank">Mercury</a> if you're running a crypto business.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep a separate crypto-free account for stability. Follow the rules. Don't be sketchy.
  </p>
  <p>
    The landscape is improving. More banks are figuring this out. Pick the right ones and you'll be fine.
  </p>
  <p>For broader nomad banking needs, see our guide to
    <a href="/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads/">banking for digital nomads</a>. Getting paid in crypto by clients? Read <a href="/tools/accept-international-payments-freelancer/">how to accept international payments</a> first. And holding several currencies at once is its own thing: <a href="/guides/multi-currency-banking-nomads/">multi-currency banking</a>.</p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
